[
    {
        "content": "<p>I am trying to figure out whether the definition of the Riemann Sphere as a complex manifold is already in mathlib. I think <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=OnePoint.equivProjectivization#doc\">docs#OnePoint.equivProjectivization</a> gives a setwise equivalence <code>OnePoint K ≃ ℙ K (Fin 2 → K)</code>, but I have not seen complex charts defined for <code>OnePoint ℂ</code> nor  <code>ℙ ℂ (Fin 2 → ℂ)</code>. I think <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40727\">PR 40727</a> might help for <code>ℙ ℂ (Fin 2 → ℂ)</code>, but I would assume that there should a more direct way for <code>OnePoint ℂ</code> just using the two charts <code>z</code> and <code>1/z</code>.</p>",
        "id": 630092853,
        "sender_full_name": "Bernhard Reinke",
        "timestamp": 1791452843
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Grassmannians/near/395947135\">#mathlib4 &gt; Grassmannians @ 💬</a> for complex charts on projective spaces.<br>\nMy previous comment at <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Geometry.20and.20complex.20dynamics.20formalization/near/615819505\">#mathlib4 &gt; Geometry and complex dynamics formalization @ 💬</a> but now I think it's probably useful to put charts on both <code>OnePoint ℂ</code> and <code>ℙ ℂ (Fin 2 → ℂ)</code> and construct the Diffeomorph between them.</p>",
        "id": 630099137,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1791454548
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>When Heather Macbeth defined the sphere as a smooth manifold, one of her design take-aways was that it's useful for formalisation to have a chart at each point: so while I agree with you mathematically, I am not sure that will be best for formalisation.</p>",
        "id": 630102834,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791455723
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My vision was to use <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40727\">#40727</a>, and define complex projective space as a quotient manifold.</p>",
        "id": 630103749,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791456019
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To get a smooth manifold structure on your favourite presentation, you could write down an equivalence, prove it's a homeomorphism, and pull back the atlas w.r.t. this structure. Then the two manifolds will be diffeomorphic: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40202\">#40202</a> starts in this direction (and is waiting on the author to get back to this).</p>",
        "id": 630104700,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791456358
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"652766\">@Christian Krause</span> Your PR is being discussed now --- I think it would still make a great addition to mathlib. If you have time to return to it, that'll be very welcome. (I'm similarly interested in seeing your branch with the proof that you get a diffeomorphism.)</p>",
        "id": 630104920,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791456428
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>My vision was to use <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40727\">#40727</a>, and define complex projective space as a quotient manifold.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>How does this work, I don't think the ℂˣ-action is properly discontinuous ... you can get real projective spaces from spheres that way though.</p>",
        "id": 630148205,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1791466498
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, what is the correct statement here? For example the group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^\\times</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">×</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> acts on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}^\\times</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">×</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and each element of the group acts via a holomorphic function but the quotient is not a complex manifold for dimension reasons.</p>",
        "id": 630150687,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1791466963
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40727\">#40727</a> covers free properly discontinuous actions so that quotient map is a covering map. If a topological action is properly discontinuous I think the group is necessarily discrete, so the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}^\\times</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">×</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>-action doesn't work, but e.g. you can quotient <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> by a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Z}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>-action and get a complex torus.</p>",
        "id": 630156583,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1791468062
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right, but we also need that if G is a complex reductive Lie group and P is a parabolic subgroup then G/P is a complex manifold. Conrete example: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GL_2(\\mathbb{C})/B=\\mathbb{P}^1_{\\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0933em;vertical-align:-0.2792em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">P</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4208em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">C</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2792em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> where <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span> is the invertible upper-triangular matrices.</p>",
        "id": 630159408,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1791468590
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree that we also want a more general case of that PR! (But I don't think it needs to block this thread.)</p>",
        "id": 630167947,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791470220
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't reductive group an algebraic notion? So you'd show the quotient is a smooth algebraic variety so the analytification is a complex manifold. (Also need that taking quotient commutes with taking closed points / taking analytification.)</p>",
        "id": 630183794,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1791473296
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes that would be one way to do it -- an algebraic group (over any field at all) can act on an algebraic variety (over the same field) and sometimes the quotient is an algebraic variety again (sometimes it is an algebraic stack), but sometimes it is not (e.g. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}^\\times</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">×</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> acts on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> by multiplication and the quotient is a pathological non-Hausdorff thing).</p>",
        "id": 630189699,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1791474391
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Bourbaki has a characterization of equivalence relations on differentiable manifolds that yield manifold quotients: see <a href=\"https://math.stackexchange.com/a/496628\">https://math.stackexchange.com/a/496628</a><br>\nHowever, I don’t know how well it extends to the analytic category.</p>",
        "id": 630292511,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1791504628
    }
]